course: dictionary
lesson: 1528
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word cereal has two meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Porridge, toast, cereal and fruit give steady energy before sport.» Here cereal is the breakfast food you eat from a bowl, with milk.
explain: Cereal can also mean a plant. Wheat, rice, maize, oats, barley and rye are cereals: grass plants whose seeds, called grains, we eat. In a farm report, «cereal crops» are the plants growing in the fields.
explain: Look at the words around it. A cereal box, a bowl of cereal and breakfast cereal all mean the food. Swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A cereal is a grass plant whose seeds, called grains, we eat.
fact: Cereal can mean a breakfast food made from grains, such as cornflakes, oats or puffed rice.
fact: Bread, pasta, rice and porridge all come from cereals.
fact: Wholegrain cereals are healthier than sugary ones, so check the label.
exercise: «Porridge, toast, cereal and fruit give steady energy before sport.» What is the cereal in this sentence? | A type of sports shoe | A grass plant growing in a field | *A breakfast food made from grains, eaten from a bowl | A kind of fruit
exercise: Which of these is a cereal crop, a grass plant whose seeds we eat? | Carrot | *Barley | Cheese | Strawberry
exercise: A 500 g cereal box holds 30 g servings. About how many servings are in the box? | *About 16 | About 5 | About 150 | About 1500
test: «Cereal at £2.40 a box costs £4.80 for two, but the "2 for £5" sign makes you pay more.» How much more does the "2 for £5" deal cost than buying two boxes at £2.40? | £2.40 | 50p | 80p | *20p
test: Which phrase uses cereal to mean the plant, not the breakfast food? | A bowl of cereal | *Cereal crops in the fields | A cereal box | Breakfast cereal
exam: What are the two meanings of cereal? | A grass plant, and a sports drink | A cereal box, and a bowl | A breakfast food, and a type of fruit | *A breakfast food made from grains, and a grass plant whose seeds we eat
